message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Beyond Gradient Descent: An LLM Agent Rewires Trained Neural Networks with Exact, Tested Patches"
abstract: >-
  Code, patches, git histories, models and paper. Gradient descent changes the values of a network's
  weights inside an architecture fixed before training. Here an LLM coding agent (Claude Opus 5.5,
  directed by a person) rewires small trained networks after training instead. It writes patches out
  of exact identities of the (Leaky)ReLU activations the networks already use, which carry, gate,
  bound and switch signals, and makes room for them by freeing channels by regression or by growing
  channels with zero outgoing weights. On four small models the patches do what training the model
  does not: a coordinate wire and an AND gate give a cat GAN odd-eyed cats while the other half of
  every image moves by at most 1.6e-7; a reference path and a min-gate repair a 2018 super-sampler
  with an output bound that holds on every input; a self-checking router resolves a fine-tune merge
  conflict (+0.23 / +3.26 / +0.00 dB, against +0.03 / +3.19 / +0.08 for joint retraining); and one
  gated unit fixes 22 of 23 MobileNetV2 cat errors while changing 1 of 1,000 other predictions.
  Patches are hash-pinned, self-testing text files that wpgit builds, tests in context, merge-checks
  and rebases under git; the repository's name, WSSE, stands for weight-space software engineering.
  Paper: https://v-code-895.github.io/wsse/
